      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.069,
            0.144,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "86"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.489,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.108,
            0.676,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "To find $( x y + x z + y z )$ , we need to use the identity"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.128,
            0.585,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": "( x + y + z ) ^ { 2 } = x ^ { 2 } + y ^ { 2 } + z ^ { 2 } + 2 x y + 2 x z + 2 y z .",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.152,
            0.749,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": "We get 100 = 38 + 2 (xy + xz + yz). Thus xy + xz + yz = 31."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.177,
            0.783,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "Now, substituting this into our earlier equation, we obtain"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.202,
            0.863,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": "x^{3}+y^{3}+z^{3}=455-10\\left ( xy+xz+yz \\right ) =455-10\\left ( 31 \\right ) =455-310=145.",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.248,
            0.646,
            0.27
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4.8 Simplifying Rational Expressions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.281,
            0.885,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": "In the earlier sections of this chapter, we have learnt how to factorise algebraic expressions. Let us see how we can simplify some rational algebraic expressions using factorisation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.338,
            0.883,
            0.388
          ],
          "content": "**Example 16:** Simplify the rational expression $\\frac{x^{2}-7x+12}{5x^{2}+5x-100}$ assuming that $5x^{2} + 5x - 100 \\ne 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.39,
            0.885,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "In order to simplify this rational expression, we need to cancel the common factors between the expressions in the numerator and denominator."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.446,
            0.885,
            0.485
          ],
          "content": "We will need to use algebraic identities to simplify this rational expression."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.485,
            0.885,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": "First, let us look at the numerator $X^{2}-7X^{\\prime \\prime}+12$ . We need to find a and b such that $a \\pm b = - 7$ and $ab = 12.$ Can you think of two such numbers? They are – 3 and – 4. Factor the expression, we get $x^{2}-7x+12=(x-3)(x-4)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.562,
            0.886,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "Now let us look at the denominator $5 \\bar { x } ^ { 2 } + 5 x - 1 0 0$ . We can see that all the three terms are multiples of 5 so we can take 5 as a common factor and simplify the expression: $5x^{2}+5x-100=5(x^{2}+x-20)$ . Now we need to find a and b such that, $a + b = 1$ and $a b = - \\; 2 0 .$ . Try to think of two such numbers. They are 5 and –4. Thus, we have $5x^{2} + 5x - 100 =$ $5 \\; ( x - 4 ) ( x + 5 )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.678,
            0.884,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": "Substituting the factored expressions in the numerator and denominator, we arrive at"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.72,
            0.764,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{x^{2}-7x+12}{5x^{2}+5x-100}=\\frac{x^{2}-7x+12}{5(x^{2}+x-20)}=\\frac{(x-4)(x-3)}{5(x-4)(x+5)}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.777,
            0.883,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the common factor $X   -   4$ can be cancelled as it is not equal to 0. We know this because $5x^{2} + 5x - 100 \\ne 0$ . Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.828,
            0.652,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{x^{2}-7x+12}{5x^{2}+5x-100}=\\frac{x-3}{5(x+5)}",
          "image_source": "images/page_18_equation_17.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
